id: k8s.desired-state
kind: kubernetes
title: Desired state
aliases: declarative configuration, desired vs observed
tags: architecture, declarative
questions:
  - what does declarative mean in Kubernetes?
invariant: You submit the desired state; controllers compare it with observed state and act on the difference.
answer_30s: You declare what should exist, not what to do; the object is the API, and controllers continuously close the gap between declared and observed state.
answer_2m: |
  Consequences that interviewers like: the same submission is idempotent (apply
  twice, one result), a deleted Pod is recreated because the declaration still
  stands, and convergence is eventually consistent rather than atomic -- which
  is why you observe status instead of assuming success. It also means the
  interesting engineering is in the diff loop, not in the command sequence, and
  that "how do I force it" becomes "what does the declaration say".
when_to_use:
  - everything you can express as state; the core of the operational model
avoid_when:
  - imperative one-off actions (use a Job, exec, or an operator that still writes desired state)
related:
  - k8s.reconciliation
  - k8s.controller
  - design.idempotency
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/controller/
